### MON-ALERT-1: ChainHeadMetricMissing scope fix

- `79e48855` — the missing-gauge alert matched `up{job="blockchain-node"}`
  (:9009) but the gauge lives on the blockchain-rpc job (:8202), so it fired
  for every literal blockchain-node target since deploy. Now matches
  `up{job=~"(.*-)?blockchain-rpc"}` and joins `on(instance, node)` —
  the node external_label disambiguates identical remote-written instance
  strings; hub-local series match the empty node value. Rule tests moved to
  live label shapes; monitoring-setup.md documents the scoping.

### MON-ALERT-2: node0 alert-state drop by label, not by name

- node0's remote_write dropped alert-state series by a 26-name alertname
  list that silently drifted as rules were added — ChainHeadMetricMissing
  was absent, so node0's evaluated copy collided with hub's own evaluation
  (duplicate-sample 400s, whole batches dropped, RemoteWriteFailing).
  node0 now drops ALERTS/ALERTS_FOR_STATE series that lack the marker
  origin=node0-local, which its local-only alerts.yml rules carry.
  Host-local files, no repo copy; documented in monitoring-setup.md.

### MON-CHECK-1: check-rules-deployed learns the host-local skip

- `8634b7eb` — the drift checker now skips listed rule files with no repo
  counterpart (matching deploy-rules.sh) and resolves absolute rule_files
  entries. node0's host-local alerts.yml no longer reports as drift;
  real drift and listed-but-undeployed still exit 1.

### ETH-20: sepolia_balance.py sends a descriptive User-Agent

- Public Sepolia RPC endpoints (publicnode, drpc, 1rpc, rpc.sepolia.org,
  rpc2.sepolia.org, blastapi) answer HTTP 403 to urllib's default
  "Python-urllib/3.x" agent, so the script's compiled-in default endpoint
  failed on every node. The request now sends "aitbc-sepolia-balance/1.0",
  which publicnode accepts (verified 200 vs 403); a unit test pins the
  non-default agent against a loopback stub.

### MSG-8g — DM read falls back across stored key eras; MCP plaintext wording corrected

- `6895b428ef` fix(messaging): `aitbc.dm_client` gains `DmDecryptError` (a `ValueError` subclass raised only for the SealedBox `CryptoError` case, same message as before) and `open_dm_with_eras()`, which tries the stored secret list newest to oldest and returns the first body that opens. Only a decrypt failure advances to the next era; a malformed ciphertext, non-UTF-8 payload or `aitbc-dm-v1` header mismatch raises on the spot — a tampered or misrouted message is never retried against older keys. When no era opens, one `ValueError` names only the tried count — no key, era or ciphertext material. `dm read` (CLI, via `_dm_key_eras`) and `MessagingClient.dm_read` (SDK) both walk the wallet's full `dm-keys` era list through the shared helper, so a DM sealed to a superseded `dm_key` still opens after `dm keygen --rotate` (MSG-VERIFY-1 G2). An explicit SDK `secret_key=` keeps its single-key meaning; corrupt stores still raise `DmStoreCorruptError` untouched; the empty-store and single-era paths are unchanged.
- Same commit, docs: the "MCP layer never sees plaintext" claim was wrong — the generated `aitbc_messaging_dm_*` tools shell out to `aitbc` on the resolved host (local subprocess or `ssh` via `_run_remote`) and return its stdout in the tool result, so DM plaintext transits the MCP server process and reaches a remote MCP client; key material is still read only on the host the CLI runs on. `docs/design/msg8-direct-messages.md` §5 and `docs/features/agent-messaging-paths.md` now say so (G4), and the design's sender-copy section records that Phase 1 implements no sender copy at all (recommendation (a) deferred — G3).
- Same commit, tests: `TestOpenDmWithEras` kernel coverage in `apps/blockchain-node/tests/test_messaging_dm.py`; CLI-era coverage (`TestDmReadEras`) in `cli/tests/test_messaging_dm.py`; SDK-era coverage in `packages/py/aitbc-sdk/tests/test_messaging_dm.py` — rotation round trip on both read paths, tried-count reporting without key/ciphertext material, header-failure no-fall-through (call-count spy), tampered-ciphertext-across-all-eras, single/empty/corrupt store parity and the explicit-`secret_key` single-attempt path.
